{0 Observe}

Observe is a portable structured logging core for OCaml. It owns admission,
formatting, diagnostics, additional drains, and deterministic capture without
performing I/O.

The current package provides tagged text, deferred text, free-form values,
typed values with Repr machine behavior and type-aware presentation, level
filtering, automatic console formatting,
drains, diagnostics, and finite capture. It does not currently provide
wide-event or metric APIs.
Drains that accept work for later effectful delivery can report a terminal
worker failure through {!val:Observe.Drain.Integration.report_failure} without
recursing through normal logging.

{1 Installation}

Link the portable core with [(libraries observe)]. Add
[(preprocess (pps observe.ppx))] when using the deriver or free-form extension.
Configurable Lwt I/O and the ready Unix composition are distributed in the
[observe-lwt] and [observe-lwt-unix] packages.

{1 Authoring}

Ordinary code logs through {!module:Observe.Logs}:

{[
Observe.Logs.info
  (Observe.Logs.text ~tag:"auth" "User logged in");

Observe.Logs.debug
  (Observe.Logs.text_lazy ~tag:"query" (fun () -> explain_query query));

Observe.Logs.info
  (Observe.Logs.free_lazy
     [%observe.value { action = "user_login"; user_id = 42 }]);

Observe.Logs.info
  (Observe.Logs.structured event_t event)
]}

Explicit lazy text, free-form, and typed thunks run only after level admission.
Before production
initialization, logs outside an active capture are withheld and counted rather
than raising an initialization exception.

{1 Configuration and output}

{!val:Observe.Config.create} returns a validated result;
{!val:Observe.Config.create_exn} raises
{!exception:Observe.Config.Invalid_configuration}. The platform console is the
automatic formatted output. {!module:Observe.Drain} values are additional
application-owned outputs that receive completed {!type:Observe.Log.t} values.
[Config.Silent] suppresses the console, not drains. [Config.Auto] chooses
pretty development output or production NDJSON from the environment.

{1 I/O composition and capture}

{!module-type:Observe.IO.S} owns effects, dynamic binding, cleanup, native
control exceptions, the wall clock, console presentation capability, and
console writes. {!module:Observe.Make} consumes that completed contract without
adding a runtime or platform dependency to the core.

{!val:Observe.Make.with_capture} installs capture as the innermost
dynamic route, suppresses production delivery for the callback, restores the
previous binding, and closes the capture exactly once.

{1 Safety boundary}

Structured values and values added with {!val:Observe.Value.embed} remain by
reference; capture is not a deep snapshot. Drains retaining asynchronous work
must synchronously copy or project what they need. Ordinary callback failures
encountered by the logging engine are contained and diagnosed, while runtime
control exceptions remain native. Arbitrary Repr descriptions do not imply
bounded traversal, cycle-safe rendering, or redaction.

Descriptions generated by [@@deriving observe] retain presentation distinctions
that JSON intentionally collapses, including strings versus nullary
constructors and ordinary versus polymorphic constructors. Observe descriptions
also carry a direct compact JSON writer for the production formatter. Decoding,
comparison, binary encoding, and the remaining machine operations stay with
Repr; {!val:Observe.Type.repr} and {!val:Observe.Type.of_repr} form the explicit
interoperability boundary.

The portable core owns no background writer or durability guarantee. The ready
Lwt-Unix package owns a bounded serialized console worker and exposes explicit
[flush] and [shutdown] lifecycle operations. See the repository README for
ready, test-capture, and custom-integration examples.
